WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.752 --> 00:00:12.549
- Growing up in Montana, I feel like I had

00:00:12.549 --> 00:00:17.041
kind of a special opportunity
just to be outside a lot.

00:00:17.041 --> 00:00:20.374
That's kind of where a love
of wildlife started for me

00:00:20.374 --> 00:00:22.344
that dies out in a lot of kids,

00:00:22.344 --> 00:00:24.046
and for me it just continued.

00:00:24.046 --> 00:00:26.239
Alright guys, so we just
finished up bear den.

00:00:26.239 --> 00:00:29.406
This little guy hasn't been named yet.

00:00:30.885 --> 00:00:33.441
My name's Wesley Larson,
I'm a masters student

00:00:33.441 --> 00:00:35.749
working in wildlife conservation,

00:00:35.749 --> 00:00:39.334
and in some circles I'm known as Griz Kid.

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:41.428
When I first started
deciding that I should get

00:00:41.428 --> 00:00:45.464
into the wildlife field, I
found Dr. Tom Smith at BYU.

00:00:45.464 --> 00:00:47.348
I started by working with polar bears

00:00:47.348 --> 00:00:50.811
on Alaska's North Slope, and
then that kind of transitioned

00:00:50.811 --> 00:00:53.997
into some black bear work down
here in Utah in Bryce Canyon.

00:00:53.997 --> 00:00:56.424
Bryce Canyon, like other national parks

00:00:56.424 --> 00:00:59.020
that do have bears, they've
had a kind of a history

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.110
with visitor conflict and with bears

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:04.123
that maybe are getting into campsites,

00:01:04.123 --> 00:01:05.948
so what we're doing is
we're tracking the bears

00:01:05.948 --> 00:01:08.117
to learn what habitats they're using,

00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:10.184
just to see what the park could do better

00:01:10.184 --> 00:01:14.347
to make it easier for
visitors to avoid bears.

00:01:14.347 --> 00:01:16.059
What we're doing is
we're fitting black bears

00:01:16.059 --> 00:01:18.184
with GPS collars, and then I'll go in

00:01:18.184 --> 00:01:22.351
to the den with the bear to
inject it with some drugs.

00:01:23.648 --> 00:01:25.822
The bear then falls
asleep, I can pull it out

00:01:25.822 --> 00:01:29.395
and you know just give it a full check up.

00:01:29.395 --> 00:01:31.820
It's probably the most scary thing we do

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.299
just because bears wake up, sometimes

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:36.688
they're really unhappy to see us.

00:01:36.688 --> 00:01:38.938
Stay, stay, stay, get back.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.153
Hey, stay.

00:01:48.131 --> 00:01:49.249
- [Narrator] More backpacks.

00:01:49.249 --> 00:01:50.244
- Get him.

00:01:50.244 --> 00:01:51.904
I have had to crawl up trees after bears.

00:01:51.904 --> 00:01:54.654
It's an interesting job for sure.

00:01:57.108 --> 00:01:58.936
People do have this knee jerk reaction

00:01:58.936 --> 00:02:00.816
to seeing an animal that's sedated

00:02:00.816 --> 00:02:02.729
or an animal that's wearing a collar

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:04.981
and think that, you know,
it might be being hurt,

00:02:04.981 --> 00:02:06.835
and the thing that they don't realize

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:08.352
is that we really care
about these animals,

00:02:08.352 --> 00:02:10.302
probably more than just about anyone.

00:02:10.302 --> 00:02:11.542
Getting out into the wilderness

00:02:11.542 --> 00:02:13.540
and having these kind of experiences

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.928
helps you understand that
we depend on wildlife

00:02:16.928 --> 00:02:18.699
and wildlife depends on
us, and that's really

00:02:18.699 --> 00:02:20.292
what conservation is all about,

00:02:20.292 --> 00:02:22.062
giving the space and the
respect to these animals

00:02:22.062 --> 00:02:24.187
that they deserve and really learning

00:02:24.187 --> 00:02:26.604
as much about them as we can.

